.TH authpf-api.conf(5) - Configuration file for authpf-api
.SH NAME
\fBauthpf-api.conf\fP — configuration file for authpf-api

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBauthpf-api.conf\fP file is a YAML-formatted configuration file for \fBauthpf-api(1)\fR\&. It defines all operational parameters including server settings, authentication, pf rule management, and role-based access control.

.PP
The default location is \fB/usr/local/etc/authpf-api.conf\fR\&. An alternative path can be specified via the \fBCONFIG_FILE\fR environment variable or the \fB-configFile\fP / \fB-c\fP command-line flags.

.PP
The whole file is validated when \fBauthpf-api(1)\fR starts and the server exits on the first invalid value, before pf is touched. An optional parameter that is empty or missing is skipped; a required parameter that is empty or missing is reported. The file itself must not be group- or world-readable: \fBLoadConfig\fR refuses it with \fIconfig file has insecure permissions\fP unless the mode is 0640 or stricter. The entries below list the accepted values and the conditions checked at startup.

.SH CONFIGURATION SECTIONS
.SS defaults
Global default settings applied across the application.
.TP
\fBpfctlBinary\fP
Path to the \fBpfctl(8)\fR binary executable (absolute path) or found in \fBPATH\fR\&. \fI(string, required)\fP

.SS authpf
AuthPF-specific configuration for rule management and timeouts.
.TP
\fBtimeout\fP
Maximum timeout for authpf anchors. Defines how long pf rules remain active before the scheduler removes them. Go duration format (\fB1s\fR, \fB30m\fR, \fB1h\fR; there is no \fBd\fR unit), at least 1 second and at most 24 hours. An unusable value aborts startup. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBuserRulesRootFolder\fP
Root directory where user-specific rule files are stored. Each user gets a subdirectory here. Must be an absolute path to an existing, readable directory. The directory is never created by \fBauthpf-api(1)\fR\&. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBuserRulesFile\fP
Filename for user rules within the \fBuserRulesRootFolder\fR\&. This file is loaded when a user activates their anchors. A plain file name only: no \fB/\fR or \fB\\\fR, no \fB\&..\fR, no leading dot, characters \fB[A-Za-z0-9_.]\fR\&. Checked at startup. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBanchorName\fP
Name of the PF anchor to use for rule management. Only the characters \fB[A-Za-z0-9_.]\fR are allowed — no \fB-\fR — because the name is passed to \fBpfctl(8)\fR as part of the anchor path. Must match the anchor name configured in \fB/etc/pf.conf\fR\&. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBflushFilter\fP
List of flush targets for \fBpfctl(8)\fR commands. Specifies which rule types to clear when flushing. Every entry is flushed separately as \fBpfctl -a <anchorName>/<user>(<userId>) -F <target>\fR\&. \fI(array of strings, required)\fP

Valid values:
  \fBnat\fR
  \fBqueue\fR
  \fBethernet\fR
  \fBrules\fR
  \fBstates\fR
  \fBinfo\fR
  \fBTables\fR
  \fBosfp\fR
  \fBall\fR

\fBSources\fR and \fBReset\fR are not accepted: they act on global pf state instead of the anchor that is always selected with \fB-a\fR\&. Whitespace, control characters, path separators and a leading \fB-\fR are rejected in any entry.

.TP
\fBonStartup\fP
Specifies the startup anchor loading behavior. \fI(string, optional)\fP

Valid values:

\fBnone\fR - Do not import anchors on startup (default)

\fBimport\fR - Import existing anchors from pf. Parses the output of \fBpfctl -sA\fR and imports all existing anchors matching the pattern \fBauthpf/NAME(USERID)\fR\&. Note: anchors without a numeric user ID suffix (e.g. \fBauthpf/user\fR without \fB(2000)\fR) are not imported.

\fBimportflush\fR — Same as \fBimport\fR, but removes/flushes all anchors immediately after importing to achieve a clean state on startup.

.TP
\fBLimitations of import:\fP

.RS
.IP "  1." 5
The \fBuser_ip\fR macro value cannot be detected during import - \fBpfctl -sA\fR does not expose anchor macros. The adopted anchor takes its address from configuration  \fBrbac.users.<name>.userIp\fR\&. If that is not configured, \fBuser_ip\fR is set to \fBunknown\fR\&. The anchor is still unloaded on deactivate, expiry and shutdown, but the address \fBauthpf.pfTable\fR holds for it cannot be revoked automatically. The API logs a warning with the manual \fBpfctl -t <table> -T delete <ip>\fR command.
.RE
.IP

.RS
.IP "  1." 5
The expiry datetime is calculated from \fBauthpf.timeout\fR and the current server time at import.
.RE

.TP
\fBonShutdown\fP
Specifies behavior when the API server shuts down. \fI(string, optional)\fP

Valid values:

\fBnone\fR — Do nothing (default)

\fBflushall\fR — Remove all activated user rules when the API server shuts down

.TP
\fBpfTable\fP
Name of a global pf table used to track active user IP addresses. When set, the user's IP is added to this table on anchor activation and removed on deactivation. The table must be declared as \fBpersist\fR in \fB/etc/pf.conf\fR before starting \fBauthpf-api(1)\fR\&. A table that does not exist aborts startup. Leave empty to disable. Can be overridden per user via \fBrbac.users.<name>.pfTable\fR\&. \fI(string, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBschedulerInterval\fP
Interval of the scheduler loop that removes expired anchors. Either a bare number of seconds (\fB60\fR) or a Go duration (\fB90s\fR, \fB2m\fR, \fB720h\fR; there is no \fBd\fR unit). Minimum 1 second, maximum 30 days; a value outside that range or an unparsable one aborts startup. Values above 30 minutes are accepted but logged as a warning, because anchors stay active up to one full interval past their own timeout. \fI(string, optional, default 60s)\fP

.SS server
Server configuration including network binding, SSL/TLS, and authentication.
.TP
\fBbind\fP
IP address to bind the server to. Use \fB127.0.0.1\fR for localhost only, or \fB0.0.0.0\fR to listen on all interfaces. Accepted are an IPv4 or IPv6 address literal (\fB0.0.0.0\fR, \fB::\fR, \fB[::1]\fR) or the name \fBlocalhost\fR\&. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBport\fP
Port number for the HTTP/HTTPS server. Ensure the port is not already in use and that the firewall allows access. \fI(integer, required)\fP

.TP
\fBssl\fP
SSL/TLS configuration for HTTPS support. \fI(object, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBssl.certificate\fP
Path to the SSL certificate file. Leave empty to disable SSL and use plain HTTP. Required for HTTPS connections. Certificate and key are parsed and matched against each other at startup. \fI(string, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBssl.key\fP
Path to the SSL private key file. Must match the certificate and be readable by the server process. Required if \fBssl.certificate\fR is specified; configuring a key without a certificate (or the other way round) will fail to start. \fI(string, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBjwtSecret\fP
JWT secret key for token signing. MUST be changed before production deployment. Use a strong, random value for security, at least 32 characters. A shorter value are not accepted. If not set or empty, a random secret is generated automatically on each startup — this means all client tokens are invalidated on restart. \fI(string, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBjwtTokenTimeout\fP
JWT token validity duration. A bare number is hours (\fB8\fR), the units \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR and \fBd\fR are also accepted (\fB30m\fR, \fB8h\fR, \fB2d\fR). Maximum is 30 days and the value must be greater than zero. Default is \fB8h\fR\&. \fI(string, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBelevatorMode\fP
Elevator mode for privilege escalation. Required when running \fBauthpf-api(1)\fR as a non-root user. Allowed values are exactly \fBnone\fR, \fBsudo\fR or \fBdoas\fR (case sensitive), and so does a configured elevator program that is not found in \fBPATH\fR\&. \fI(string, required)\fP

Valid values:

\fBnone\fR — No privilege escalation (default). Only use if running as root.

\fBsudo\fR — Use \fBsudo(8)\fR for privilege escalation. Requires appropriate sudoers configuration.

\fBdoas\fR — Use \fBdoas(1)\fR for privilege escalation. Requires appropriate doas.conf configuration.

.TP
\fBlogfile\fP
Path to the server logfile. Relative paths are allowed. The file is opened (created, mode 0640) on every startup, also when started with \fB-foreground\fR, so a wrong path or an unwritable directory aborts startup. \fB-foreground\fR still logs to stdout. \fI(string, required)\fP

.SS rbac
Role-Based Access Control configuration for users and permissions.
.TP
\fBrbac.roles\fP
Role definitions with associated permissions. Each role defines what actions users with that role can perform. \fI(object, required)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.roles.<name>\&.permissions\fP
List of permission strings assigned to this role. Values are listed under \fBAVAILABLE RBAC PERMISSIONS\fP . An empty list is allowed and duplicated entries are ignored. \fI(array of strings, required)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.users\fP
User account definitions with credentials and role assignments. At least one user is required. User names must match \fB[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+\fR and be at most 255 characters. \fI(object, required)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.users.<name>\&.password\fP
Bcrypt password hash for this user. Generate with \fBauthpf-api -gen-user-password\fR\&. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.users.<name>\&.role\fP
Role assigned to this user. Must be a non-empty key defined under \fBrbac.roles\fR\&. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.users.<name>\&.userId\fP
Numeric user ID used as the pf anchor suffix (e.g. \fBauthpf/username(1001)\fR). Defaults to \fB0\fR if not set. Must not be negative; positive values must be unique across all users. \fI(integer, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.users.<name>\&.userIp\fP
Pin a static IP address passed as the \fBuser_ip\fR macro to \fBpfctl\fR\&. If omitted, the remote client IP of the HTTP request is used automatically. When set, the configured value is always used regardless of the actual source IP. This is also the address the API assigns to an anchor adopted by \fBauthpf.onStartup: import\fR, since pf does not report the macro of an already loaded anchor. \fI(string, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.users.<name>\&.macros\fP
Map of arbitrary key/value pairs passed as additional \fB-D key=value\fR arguments to \fBpfctl\fR when the user's anchor is loaded. Each macro must also be declared in the user's pf rules file. Macro keys must match \fB[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]*\fR, macro values may contain \fB[A-Za-z0-9_.]\fR, both 1 to 128 characters. The keys \fBuser_ip\fR and \fBuser_id\fR are reserved by the application and rejected. \fI(map of string to string, optional)\fP

.TP
\fBrbac.users.<name>\&.pfTable\fP
Override the global \fBauthpf.pfTable\fR for this specific user. If set, this user's IP is tracked in this table instead of the global one. The table must be declared as \fBpersist\fR in \fB/etc/pf.conf\fR\&. \fI(string, optional)\fP

.SS extensions
Optional list of extensions to load, each with a \fBname\fR and an optional \fBconfig\fR block.
.TP
\fBextensions[].name\fP
Name of the extension to load, as reported by \fBauthpf-api -list-extensions\fR\&. Must match \fB^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$\fR and may be listed only once. \fI(string, required)\fP

.TP
\fBextensions[].config\fP
Free-form key/value block passed unchanged to the \fBInit()\fR of the extension. These keys are not validated by \fBauthpf-api\fR: each extension checks its own configuration and either reports a bad key at startup or keeps its default for it. \fI(object, optional)\fP

.SH AVAILABLE RBAC PERMISSIONS
.TP
\fBactivate_own_rules\fP
Allow user to activate their own authpf rules.

.TP
\fBactivate_other_rules\fP
Allow user to activate rules for other users (via \fBauthpf_username\fR query parameter).

.TP
\fBdeactivate_own_rules\fP
Allow user to deactivate their own rules.

.TP
\fBdeactivate_other_rules\fP
Allow user to deactivate rules for other users (via \fBauthpf_username\fR query parameter). Also required for \fBDELETE /api/v1/authpf/all\fR\&.

.TP
\fBview_own_rules\fP
Allow user to view their own rules status via \fBGET /api/v1/authpf/all\fR\&.

.TP
\fBview_other_rules\fP
Allow user to view rules status for all users via \fBGET /api/v1/authpf/all\fR\&.

.SH PF TABLE SUPPORT
\fBauthpf-api\fP can automatically track active user IPs in a pf table, similar to the original \fBauthpf(8)\fR behaviour. This allows pf rules to reference the set of currently authenticated users by table name.

.PP
When a user's anchor is \fBactivated\fP, their \fBuser_ip\fR is added to the configured pf table via \fBpfctl -t <table> -T add <ip>\fR\&. When a user's anchor is \fBdeactivated\fP, their IP is removed via \fBpfctl -t <table> -T delete <ip>\fR\&. On startup, \fBauthpf-api\fP verifies that all configured tables exist — this check is fatal and prevents the server from starting if a table is missing.

.PP
\fBPriority\fP (highest to lowest):

.EX
rbac.users.<name>.pfTable  →  authpf.pfTable  →  no table management
.EE

.PP
The table must be declared as \fBpersist\fR in \fB/etc/pf.conf\fR:

.EX
table <authpf_users> persist
.EE

.PP
Without \fBpersist\fR, pf removes the table when it becomes empty, causing subsequent \fBpfctl -T add\fR calls to fail.

.PP
\fBError behaviour:\fP

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBOperation\fP	\fBOn failure\fP
Add IP on activate	T{
Fatal — HTTP 500 returned, anchor is not activated
T}
Remove IP on deactivate	T{
Warn only — logged, anchor is still flushed
T}
T{
Remove IP on shutdown / deactivate-all
T}	T{
Warn only — logged, all anchors are still flushed
T}
T{
Table existence check on startup
T}	T{
Fatal — server refuses to start
T}
.TE

.SH PASSWORD GENERATION
User passwords must be hashed using bcrypt. The \fB-gen-user-password\fR flag performs a two-step hash: SHA256 followed by bcrypt. The API login endpoint expects the password to be sent as a SHA256 hash.

.EX
authpf-api -gen-user-password
Enter password:
$2a$10$N9qo8uLOickgx2ZM.......
.EE

.PP
Or via pipe:

.EX
echo -n "your-password" | authpf-api -gen-user-password
$2a$10$N9qo8uLOickgx2ZM.......
.EE

.PP
Copy the generated hash and add it to the configuration file under \fBrbac.users.<name>.password\fR\&.

.SH ELEVATOR SETUP
When running \fBauthpf-api\fP as a non-root user, an elevator setup is required. \fBauthpf-api\fP supports both \fBsudo(8)\fR and \fBdoas(1)\fR\&.

.SS Configure Sudo
Add the following to the sudoers file:

.EX
Cmnd_Alias AUTHPF_API_COMMANDS = /sbin/pfctl -sA, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? \\
        -D user_ip=[0-9.]+ -D user_id=[0-9]+ \\
        -f /etc/authpf/users/[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+/authpf.rules$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? -F nat$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? -F rules$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? -F queue$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? -F states$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? -F Tables$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? -F all$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-t [a-zA-Z0-9_]+ -T add [0-9a-fA-F:.]+$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-t [a-zA-Z0-9_]+ -T delete [0-9a-fA-F:.]+$, \\
    /sbin/pfctl ^-t [a-zA-Z0-9_]+ -T show$
%_authpf-api ALL=(root)  NOPASSWD: AUTHPF_API_COMMANDS
.EE

.PP
The three pf table rules (\fB-T add\fR, \fB-T delete\fR, \fB-T show\fR) are only required when \fBauthpf.pfTable\fR or any \fBrbac.users.<name>.pfTable\fR is configured. They cover both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses.

.PP
When using user-defined \fBmacros\fR, the sudoers \fBCmnd_Alias\fR must be updated to allow the additional \fB-D\fR arguments. Use a broad regex pattern to cover all macro key/value pairs:

.EX
/sbin/pfctl ^-a authpf/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(\\([0-9]+\\))? \\
    -D [a-zA-Z0-9_-]+=[a-zA-Z0-9_.+-]+ \\
    -f /etc/authpf/users/[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+/authpf.rules$
.EE

.SS Configure Doas
Add the following to \fB/etc/doas.conf\fR:

.EX
permit nopass :authpf as root cmd /sbin/pfctl
.EE

.PP
For a more restrictive setup, use the \fBpfctl_wrapper\fR script (found under \fBscripts/\fR) and configure \fBdefaults.pfctlBinary\fR to point to it:

.EX
permit nopass :authpf as root cmd /usr/local/sbin/pfctl_wrapper
.EE

.PP
\fBNote:\fP \fBdoas(1)\fR does not support regular expressions for command arguments. The broad \fBcmd /sbin/pfctl\fR rule allows all pfctl invocations. Use the \fBpfctl_wrapper\fR for tighter control.

.PP
\fBNote:\fP On FreeBSD 15.0, \fBpfctl -Fa\fR (flush all) may return an error (\fBOperation not supported by device\fR) due to a NETLINK change. Run each flush filter separately instead of using \fBall\fR\&.

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Minimal Configuration
.EX
defaults:
  pfctlBinary: /sbin/pfctl

authpf:
  timeout: 30m
  userRulesRootFolder: /etc/authpf/users
  userRulesFile: authpf.rules
  anchorName: authpf
  flushFilter:
    - nat
    - rules
  onStartup: none
  onShutdown: none

server:
  bind: 127.0.0.1
  port: 8080
  logfile: /var/log/authpf-api.log

rbac:
  roles:
    user:
      permissions:
        - activate_own_rules
        - deactivate_own_rules
        - view_own_rules
  users:
    authpf-user1:
      password: $2a$10$abcdefg.....
      role: user
.EE

.SS Production Configuration with SSL, Sudo, and PF Table
.EX
defaults:
  pfctlBinary: /sbin/pfctl

authpf:
  timeout: 1h
  userRulesRootFolder: /etc/authpf/users
  userRulesFile: authpf.rules
  anchorName: authpf
  flushFilter:
    - nat
    - queue
    - rules
  onStartup: importflush
  onShutdown: flushall
  pfTable: authpf_users

server:
  bind: 0.0.0.0
  port: 443
  ssl:
    certificate: /etc/ssl/certs/authpf-api.crt
    key: /etc/ssl/private/authpf-api.key
  jwtSecret: your-strong-random-secret-here-change-me
  jwtTokenTimeout: 12h
  elevatorMode: sudo
  logfile: /var/log/authpf-api.log

rbac:
  roles:
    admin:
      permissions:
        - activate_own_rules
        - activate_other_rules
        - deactivate_own_rules
        - deactivate_other_rules
        - view_own_rules
        - view_other_rules
    user:
      permissions:
        - activate_own_rules
        - deactivate_own_rules
        - view_own_rules
  users:
    authpf-admin:
      password: $2a$10$abcdefg.....
      role: admin
      userId: 1000
    authpf-user1:
      password: $2a$10$hijklmni.....
      role: user
      userId: 1001
      userIp: 192.168.0.10
      pfTable: custom_table_user1
      macros:
        server_1_port: 22
        server_1_addr: 10.127.2.1
.EE

.SH FILES
.EX
/usr/local/etc/authpf-api.conf — Default configuration file location
/etc/authpf/users              — Default root directory for user-specific rule files
/etc/pf.conf                   — Packet filter configuration file
/var/log/authpf-api.log        — Default logfile location
.EE

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBCONFIG_FILE\fP
Override the default configuration file path. Overridden by the \fB-configFile\fP / \fB-c\fP flags.

.TP
\fBLOG_LEVEL\fP
Set the logging level (\fBdebug\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBwarn\fR, \fBerror\fR, \fBfatal\fR). Overridden by the \fB-v\fP flag.

.SH SEE ALSO
authpf-api(1),
authpf(8),
pfctl(8),
pf(4),
pf.conf(5),
sudo(8),
doas(1)

.SH HISTORY
The \fBauthpf-api.conf\fP configuration file format was introduced with \fBauthpf-api(1)\fR as a YAML-based alternative to traditional shell-based authpf configuration.

